    {
      "id": "ES-C11-querer",
      "sequence": 1060,
      "title": "querer — wanting with one familiar vowel change",
      "headword": "querer",
      "romanization": "querer",
      "gloss": "to want — with the singular present forms quiero, quieres, and quiere",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:fecf75d794ce8eb4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You already say tienes and tiene, where the middle e opens into ie. Today, reuse that visible change in one useful verb and keep the same three-person frame you already own."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "diphthong-ie — begin quiero with one smooth kye- syllable."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-tap — end the infinitive querer with one light tap."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "querer means “to want.” With a person as its object, it can also express love, but wanting is the meaning used in this lesson."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It descends from Latin quaerere, “to seek or ask.” English keeps the same searching idea in query, quest, inquire, and require. Wanting something begins as seeking it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: only the three singular forms",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "present of querer"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. present of querer: quiero.",
                "person: tú. present of querer: quieres.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. present of querer: quiere."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the small set as quiero, quieres, quiere. The e becomes ie that you saw in tienes, tiene is visible again. The plural persons are deliberately left for a later step."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Place one known infinitive after the form of querer:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Quiero hablar. — “I want to speak.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Quieres comer. — “You want to eat.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Quiere vivir. — “He, she, or formal you wants to live.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The second verb stays in its known infinitive form."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
