    {
      "id": "ES-C11-poder",
      "sequence": 1070,
      "title": "poder — ability with the o becomes ue change",
      "headword": "poder",
      "romanization": "poder",
      "gloss": "to be able or can — with the singular present forms puedo, puedes, and puede",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4bf7adb0fa0c98c9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Quiero hablar says what you want to do. Now change the first verb to say what you are able to do. The second verb will remain a known infinitive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "diphthong-ue — puedo begins pwe- with one smooth glide."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-tap — end poder with one light tap."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "poder means “to be able”; in a sentence it often translates as “can.” It descends through Latin potere from posse, “to be able.” English power, potent, potential, and possible belong to the same family. Ability is the power to do something."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the three singular forms",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "present of poder"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. present of poder: puedo.",
                "person: tú. present of poder: puedes.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. present of poder: puede."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say puedo, puedes, puede. In these three learned forms, the stem o opens into ue. Plural persons wait."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Follow the form of poder with one unchanged infinitive:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puedo hablar. — “I can speak.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puedes comer. — “You can eat.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puede vivir. — “He, she, or formal you can live.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Compare Quiero hablar with Puedo hablar: only the first verb changes the meaning from want to ability."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
