    {
      "id": "ES-C11-stem-changes",
      "sequence": 1080,
      "title": "e becomes ie and o becomes ue — compare the singular forms",
      "headword": "e → ie and o → ue",
      "romanization": "e → ie and o → ue",
      "gloss": "comparing the two vowel changes inside the learned singular forms",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d2838702fc3ab39b",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You now own three small singular sets: tengo, tienes, tiene; quiero, quieres, quiere; and puedo, puedes, puede. Compare only those learned forms before extending the pattern later."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart — why vowels opened",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "On the road from Latin to Spanish, many short e and o vowels opened when stressed: e becomes ie and o becomes ue. That history helps explain why a verb's stem can look different across its forms."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is a clue, not a machine that predicts every form. Learn each verb's small set, then use the shared history to organize what you remember."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: two changes, one learned frame",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "tener / querer, e becomes ie family",
                "poder, o becomes ue"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. tener / querer, e becomes ie family: tengo / quiero. poder, o becomes ue: puedo.",
                "person: tú. tener / querer, e becomes ie family: tienes / quieres. poder, o becomes ue: puedes.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. tener / querer, e becomes ie family: tiene / quiere. poder, o becomes ue: puede."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The comparison says exactly this:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "querer shows ie in all three forms learned here."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "poder shows ue in all three forms learned here."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tener reminds you that a common verb may combine more than one change: tengo has its known g, while tienes and tiene show ie."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No plural form and no new verb is part of today's generalization."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "“quiero, quieres, quiere”",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: “quiero, quieres, quiere”]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "“puedo, puedes, puede”",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: “puedo, puedes, puede”]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "“stressed e and o often opened to ie and ue”",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: “stressed e and o often opened to ie and ue”]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C11-stem-changes-yo-contrast",
              "prompt": "Give the learned yo forms of querer and poder, in that order.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-STEM-CHANGE-CONTRAST-SINGULAR",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-QUERER-PRESENT-SINGULAR",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PODER-PRESENT-SINGULAR"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "quiero, puedo",
                "quiero puedo",
                "quiero / puedo"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right: quiero, puedo — e becomes ie beside o becomes ue.",
                "incorrect": "Use the learned yo forms: quiero, then puedo."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C11-stem-changes-tu-contrast",
              "prompt": "Give the learned tú forms of tener, querer, and poder, in that order.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-STEM-CHANGE-CONTRAST-SINGULAR",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-TENER-PRESENT-SINGULAR",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-QUERER-PRESENT-SINGULAR",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PODER-PRESENT-SINGULAR"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "tienes, quieres, puedes",
                "tienes quieres puedes",
                "tienes / quieres / puedes"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right: tienes, quieres, puedes.",
                "incorrect": "Retrieve the three known tú forms: tienes, quieres, puedes."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which change appears in querer? (e becomes ie.) In poder? (o becomes ue.) Why is the history useful but not a complete prediction rule? (It organizes learned forms, but individual verbs can combine or resist changes.) Next: one possessive that agrees with a known noun's gender."
            }
          ]
        }
