      "title": "nuestro, nuestra — “our” with singular gender agreement",
      "headword": "nuestro, nuestra",
      "romanization": "nuestro, nuestra",
      "gloss": "our — matching a known masculine or feminine singular noun",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9586b88ff6abb158",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You put mi, tu, or su before the known noun café. Now add “our” and let it match one contrast you learned much earlier: masculine día beside feminine noche."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nuestro and nuestra mean “our.” They descend from Latin noster, also “our.” Paternoster means “Our Father,” and English nostrum originally meant “our thing.”"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The change from noster to nuestro also displays the historical o becomes ue opening you just used to organize poder."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: match singular gender",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "known noun",
                "“our” phrase"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "known noun: el día, masculine. “our” phrase: nuestro día.",
                "known noun: la noche, feminine. “our” phrase: nuestra noche."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Like mi, tu, and su, the new word goes before the noun. Unlike those earlier forms, it shows the noun's gender: nuestro before a masculine singular noun, nuestra before a feminine singular noun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Only these two singular forms belong to this lesson. Plural agreement and the separate word for “your” to a group wait."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
